s showing a tie temper the picture slightly, but markets and most raters treat this as well out of reach for Walz.", "watch": "If independent pollsters begin confirming the single-digit or tied numbers seen in Democratic-sponsored surveys, the race could tighten into genuine contention.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Incumbent Jim Pillen (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Republican Governor Jim Pillen seeks a second term against Democratic state senator Lynne Walz, with two minor candidates also on the ballot. Nebraska's Republican lean and Pillen's incumbency form the backbone of his advantage, and most expert raters call the race Solid Republican, with a couple rating it Likely Republican.\n\nRecent polling is inconsistent. Neutral pollsters in September showed Pillen ahead by roughly 5 to 10 points, while Democratic-aligned surveys found the race tied or within a point, and headlines have described a statistical dead heat. Prediction markets remain skeptical of an upset, pricing Walz around 3 to 10 percent. Both campaigns are on the air, and Walz has publicized Republican endorsements while Pillen has secured groups like the NRA. The weight of evidence still favors Pillen by high single digits, with enough polling noise to keep some uncertainty.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.0219, "margin": -11.34}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.0357, "margin": -11.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.0267, "margin": -11.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.0322, "margin": -11.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.0312, "margin": -11.5}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.0585, "margin": -10.0}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.0639, "margin": -10.0}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.0714, "margin": -9.0}, {"date": "2026-09-30", "p_dem": 0.0694, "margin": -8.5}, {"date": "2026-10-01", "p_dem": 0.0605, "margin": -9.0}, {"date": "2026-10-02", "p_dem": 0.0982, "margin": -8.5}, {"date": "2026-10-03", "p_dem": 0.0987, "margin": -8.0}, {"date": "2026-10-04